    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "The test on L104 should be the same as this one. Indeed, selectedItem can be either a non-empty string, in which case we consider that there is a selected item whose value is `selectedItem`, or an empty string or null, and in both cases we consider that there is no selected item. I'm not sure why I kept null as a possible value, as the default value should always be `''`.\n\nThe reason why the explicit absence of value must be represented as an empty string in state is because the state gets serialized to an URL:\n- `{q: 'hello', category: null}` serializes to `?q=hello`, which deserializes to `{q: 'hello'}`: we lose information.\n- `{q: 'hello', category: ''}` serializes to `?category=&q=hello`, which deserializes to the original `{q: 'hello', category: ''}`\n\nSo in the case where we set `category` to `null`, we lose the knowledge that the state of the `category` widget was explicitly reset. This only has consequences when the user of the library provides a default state for the `category` widget.\n1. `const defaultState = {category: 'defaultCategory'};`\n2. The state is initialized from the url.  \n   `?q=hello` -> `{...defaultState, q: 'hello'}` -> `{q: 'hello', category: 'defaultCategory'}`\n3. The user explicitly deselects the `defaultCategory` category.  \n   `{q: 'hello', category: ''}` -> `'?category=&q=hello` -> `{...defaultState, q: 'hello', category: ''}` -> `{q: 'hello', category: ''}`\n\nDoes that make sense?\n\nFrom the looks of it, this behaviour might be broken in the current version. I've updated this widget in a future PR (for tests), so maybe I should try to fix it there?\n",
    "commit_id": "0609244f5a883922e4e863e9acba8179e0b6148c",
